      "text": "Since $f(0)=0$, then $f\\left( 1-\\frac{1}{0}\\right)=f(0)=0$. In other words, $f$ has undefined value at $x=0$, just as it does at 1. Next, let $x=2$. Then we get \n\\begin{align*}\nf(2)+f\\left( 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)&= \\log2\\\\ \\\\\nf(2)+f\\left( \\frac{1}{2}\\right)&= \\log2\\\\\n\\end{align*}\nBecause $\\log2 - \\log(\\frac{1}{2}) = 2 $ and $f(2) = f(\\frac{1}{2}) = 2$, we see that we must have $f(2) = \\log2$. Now, let $x=3$. Then $f(3)+f(1-\\frac{1}{3})=\\log3$. Because $f(3)$ is undefined, however, we cannot write it down. Let's instead consider a different function, one we can safely define at 3. Define $g(3)$ to be whatever we can find that $g(3) + f(1-\\frac{1}{3}) = \\log3$ when $f(1-\\frac{1}{3})$ is defined already. Because $g(3)$ must be rational, so must $\\log3 - g(3)$. But already at $x=1$ we ensure that $\\log3-\\log(1-x) = -\\log(x-1)$ is horizontal for $0 < x < 1$, which means that $f(1-x)$ must take only integer values otherwise we'd have a periodic relationship between $\\log3-\\log(1-x)$ and $\\log3+\\log(1-x)$ in a better harmony not allowed. Since $g(3)$ is probably the only required undefined value for the function, and $g(3)$ must be minimally rational, we can assume without loss of generality that $g(3) = -1$. Therefore, the function is in effect\n\\begin{align*}\nf(3)&=-1\\\\ \nf(4)&= \\log2 -\\log(1-\\frac{1}{3}) = \\log2+1\\\\ \nf(5)&= \\log3 - \\log(1-\\frac{1}{2.5}) = \\log3+1\\\\\nf(n)&= \\log n + 1 \\qquad \\text{for integers } n > 2\\\\\n\\end{align*}\nAnd thus $f(2007) = \\log2007 + 1$.\n\nWe can know $\\log a + \\log b = \\log(ab)$ hence only $2007$ and $1$ are prime number so $\\log 2007 +1 = \\log 2007$",
